course: everyday
lesson: 13
title: Smells that warn
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Your nose is an early-warning system: it can pick up tiny amounts of some substances in the air long before you can see anything.
explain: A smell of smoke can mean something is burning; act on it even if you see no flames.
explain: Natural gas for cooking and heating has no smell of its own, so gas companies add a chemical that smells like rotten eggs, just so people notice a leak.
explain: Spoiled food often smells sour or bad because bacteria and moulds grow in it; but some germs that make you ill give no smell, so a good smell does not prove food is safe.
explain: Example: you walk into a kitchen and smell rotten eggs — do not touch the light switch; leave, and tell an adult from outside.
explain: A family in Boston had a boiler that leaked carbon monoxide, a gas with no smell; they kept getting headaches and feeling sleepy until a carbon monoxide alarm went off and they got out in time.
explain: A girl in Wales smelt gas in the kitchen and switched on the light to look; a spark could have set the gas alight, so now she knows to get out first and call from outside.
fact: Natural gas has no smell, so a strong-smelling chemical is added to make leaks noticeable.
fact: Leaking gas smells like rotten eggs.
fact: A spark from a light switch, a lighter or a match could set leaked gas alight.
fact: If you smell gas, do not touch switches; leave the building and call for help from outside.
fact: Hot smoke rises, so in a fire the cleaner air is near the floor.
fact: If you smell smoke, tell an adult at once, and get out if it is not clearly safe.
fact: Carbon monoxide has no smell, which is why homes need carbon monoxide alarms.
fact: Spoiled food may smell sour or rotten because bacteria (tiny living things too small to see) and moulds are breaking it down.
fact: Some bacteria that cause food poisoning do not change how food smells or looks.
fact: A safe rule for doubtful food is: when in doubt, throw it out.
fact: After you smell something for a while, your nose gets used to it and you notice it less.
fact: Some cleaning products give off a poisonous gas when mixed, so they must never be mixed.
fact: Carbon monoxide has no smell or colour, and it can make people sleepy, sick and even kill them.
fact: A gas leak can explode if a flame or spark lights it.
exercise: Why do gas companies add a smell to natural gas? | So people notice a leak, as the gas has no smell | Because natural gas smells of rotten eggs anyway, and they want it stronger | To make the flames on the cooker burn brighter | So the gas burns more slowly and safely
exercise: What does leaking gas smell like? | Rotten eggs | Burnt toast | Fresh paint | Lemon cleaner
exercise: Why should you not switch on a light if you smell gas? | A spark could set the gas alight | Light makes the gas spread faster | The light will hide the smell | Electricity turns gas into smoke
exercise: You smell gas at home. What should you do? | Leave and call for help from outside | Light a match to find the leak | Open the oven door to check it | Stay inside and phone from the kitchen
exercise: Why should you crawl low if there is smoke in a room? | Hot smoke rises, so cleaner air is near the floor | Smoke sinks, so the air is better up high near the ceiling | Crawling makes the fire spread more slowly | Fire cannot reach things close to the floor
exercise: Why do homes need carbon monoxide alarms? | The gas has no smell, so noses cannot warn us | The gas smells too strong for people to bear for long | The gas is only made by broken alarms | Noses can smell it only in summer
exercise: Why does spoiled milk smell sour? | Bacteria growing in it make sour substances | The cold of the fridge turns it sour | Milk turns sour when light touches it | Milk turns sour as soon as the carton opens
exercise: Can you be sure food is safe because it smells fine? | No, some harmful germs do not change the smell | Yes, harmful germs always make food smell bad, every time | Yes, if it also looks fine it is always safe | No, food that smells fine is always unsafe
exercise: What does «when in doubt, throw it out» mean? | If you are not sure food is safe, do not eat it | Throw out all food after one day, even if it is fresh | Eat food quickly before it can spoil | Throw out only food that smells bad
exercise: Why do you stop noticing a smell after a while in the same room? | Your nose gets used to it | The smell always disappears quickly | Your nose stops working indoors | Smells only last for one minute
exercise: How could your nose getting used to a smell be dangerous? | You might stop noticing slowly leaking gas | It could make your nose start to bleed | It makes food taste much too salty | It makes you smell things that are not there
exercise: Why should you never mix different cleaning products? | Some mixtures give off a poisonous gas | Mixing makes them clean twice as well | Mixed cleaners turn into plain water | Mixing them makes the bottles melt
exercise: You smell smoke at school but see no fire. What should you do? | Tell a teacher at once | Ignore it unless you see flames | Go and look for the fire yourself | Wait to see if it gets stronger
exercise: Why is a smell often the first warning of a fire? | Smoke spreads through the air before you see flames | Fires always start with a smell but never with any smoke | Flames are invisible until they grow large | Noses can smell heat from far away
test: Leo comes home and the hallway smells of rotten eggs. He reaches for the light switch. What should he do instead? | Leave the switch alone and go back outside | Switch on the light to look for the leak | Open the cooker to check the knobs | Light a candle so that he can see
test: Which is true about natural gas? | It has no smell until a smelly chemical is added | It smells of rotten eggs all by itself, straight from the ground | It smells sweet, like flowers | Only dogs are able to smell it
test: A girl finds cooked chicken in the fridge from a week ago. It smells fine. What should she think? | It may still be unsafe, so better thrown out | It is safe to eat because it smells fine | It is safe to eat if she eats it cold | It is safe if she sniffs it a second time and it still smells fine
test: Why is carbon monoxide called a silent danger? | You cannot see, smell or taste it | It makes no sound when it burns | It only appears at night | It is too heavy to move around
test: A room is filling with smoke. Where is the best air? | Near the floor | Near the ceiling | On top of a table | Up by the light
test: An adult is mixing two cleaners and a sharp smell fills the bathroom. What should happen? | Stop, open the windows and leave the room | Keep mixing to make it stronger and clean better | Sniff it closely to check the smell | Add hot water and stir it faster
test: If no smell were added to gas, what would happen? | People would not notice leaks | Gas would stop burning | Cookers would need no flame | Leaks would be noticed sooner
test: Why do fire safety lessons say to get out first and phone afterwards? | Staying in to call wastes precious time | Phones only work outdoors | Calls from inside go to the wrong place | The fire stops while you are on the phone
test: After an hour in a freshly painted room, Jack can no longer smell the paint. Why? | His nose has got used to the smell | The paint has stopped smelling | He has caught a cold from it | The smell has all gone up to the ceiling
test: kitchen smells like rotten eggs, turn the light on? | No, leave and call for help from outside | Yes, so you can find the leak | Yes, then open the oven | Light a match so you can see the cooker better
test: milk smells fine but it's way past the date, drink it? | When in doubt, throw it out | Yes, smell is always a safe test | Yes, if it looks white | Yes, just boil it a bit
test: can i mix bleach n the other cleaner to make it stronger | No, some mixes give off poisonous gas | Yes, it cleans twice as well | Yes, if you use cold water | Only in a small bucket
test: smell smoke upstairs, what do i do | Tell an adult and get out if unsure | Go and look for the fire alone | Open all the windows first | Wait and see if it gets worse
test: kitchen smells like rotten eggs, mum's out | Get out and call for help from outside | Light a candle to cover the smell | Turn on the lights to look for it | Open the oven to check it, then ring mum at work
test: whole family's got headaches and feels sleepy, the co alarm's beeping | Get everyone out into fresh air and call for help | Take a nap and see if it passes | Turn the alarm off and open one window in the kitchen a bit | Make tea and sit down together
test: Ben says if u can't smell anything there's no gas danger. true? | No, carbon monoxide has no smell at all | Yes, all dangerous gases smell | Yes, gas always smells like smoke | Yes, unless you have a cold and a blocked nose
test: did u hear the Patels' flat nearly blew up cos someone flicked the light on when it smelled of gas, the windows came out | Real lesson: if you smell gas, touch no switches and get out; «windows came out» may be exaggerated | Real lesson: lights are always dangerous to switch on | It proves gas only explodes in flats | It is gossip, so a gas smell is nothing to worry about
test: my mate says you'd smell carbon monoxide straight away, it smells like burning | That is false; carbon monoxide has no smell, which is why homes need alarms | He is right, it smells like smoke | He is right, it smells like rotten eggs | He is right, but only at night
test: someone's spreading that Ella's lunch box stinks cos her family's dirty, it's going round | Do not pass it on; it is cruel and probably untrue, and it hurts her | Pass it on, because a smell is a fact | Pass it on, so she brings different food | Pass it on, but only to her friends
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the odour associated with a leak of domestic gas? | Natural gas being odourless, a strong-smelling substance resembling rotten eggs is added to it | Natural gas possesses of itself a strong odour of rotten eggs | Natural gas smells faintly of flowers | A leak of gas produces no detectable odour of any kind
exam: It being given that an odour of gas is perceived within a dwelling, select the response consistent with established guidance. | To refrain from operating switches, to leave the building, and to summon assistance from outside | To switch on the lights in order to locate the leak | To light a match so as to burn away the gas | To telephone for help from within the kitchen
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning food of doubtful condition. | Some harmful bacteria alter neither smell nor appearance, wherefore doubtful food should be discarded | Food that smells normal is invariably safe to consume | Food spoiled by bacteria always smells sour | Heating spoiled food always renders it safe
exam: Identify the principal reason for which cleaning products must never be mixed together. | Certain mixtures give off a poisonous gas | The mixture invariably ceases to clean | The mixture becomes excessively sticky | The containers are rendered unfit for recycling
